  100vw
  " srcSet="https://assets.babycenter.com/ims/2020/04/iStock-680677544_wide.jpg?width=80 80w, https://assets.babycenter.com/ims/2020/04/iStock-680677544_wide.jpg?width=150 150w, https://assets.babycenter.com/ims/2020/04/iStock-680677544_wide.jpg?width=300 300w, https://assets.babycenter.com/ims/2020/04/iStock-680677544_wide.jpg?width=396 396w, https://assets.babycenter.com/ims/2020/04/iStock-680677544_wide.jpg?width=650 650w, https://assets.babycenter.com/ims/2020/04/iStock-680677544_wide.jpg?width=850 850w, https://assets.babycenter.com/ims/2020/04/iStock-680677544_wide.jpg?width=1200 1200w" src="https://assets.babycenter.com/ims/2020/04/iStock-680677544_wide.jpg?width=1200"/><div class="caption">Photo credit:<!-- --> <!-- -->iStock.com / SolStock</div></div><div class="bodyText authoredContentDefaultStyles linksWithNewWindowIndicator expertText mui-jpjv2o"><p>You&#x27;re moving along the main street, minding your own business when suddenly – bam! You get hit from behind. It&#x27;s jarring when you&#x27;re <em>not</em> pregnant, but when you&#x27;re expecting, the anxiety is even worse.</p> <p>Any time you&#x27;re in a car accident while you&#x27;re pregnant – whether it&#x27;s a minor fender-bender or a more serious collision – it&#x27;s best to be examined by a healthcare provider as soon as possible, no matter how far along you are in your pregnancy.</p><div class="cmsAdContainer mui-fx7q5a"><div class="greyAdBox mui-1qwcnd7"><div class="advertisementLabel grey">Advertisement | page continues below</div><div class="adSlotContainer grey"></div></div></div> <div class="takeaways"> <h3>Key Takeaways</h3> <ul> <li>After an accident, evaluate how you&#x27;re feeling. If you&#x27;re concerned at all about your health, have someone drive you straight to the ER or call an ambulance.</li> <li>If you feel alright, still call your healthcare provider, explain the situation, and follow their advice.</li> </ul> </div> <h2 id="what-to-do-immediately-after-a-car-accident">What to do immediately after a car accident</h2> <p>Take a second to breathe, reassure yourself that you&#x27;re okay, and evaluate how you&#x27;re feeling. If you have any concerns about your body, your pregnancy, or you feel like you or your baby may be injured or in distress, go straight to the ER. Call 911 or have your partner or someone else take you there.</p> <p>&quot;The first concern of emergency medical personnel is the well-being of the pregnant woman,&quot; says <a href="/expert-rebecca-amaru">Dr. Rebecca Amaru</a>, a New York-based ob-gyn. &quot;Her life is not more valuable because she is pregnant, and her first concern can and should be her own health. What that means is that when she is in an accident, the ABCs [Airway, Breathing, and Circulation] of EMS care should focus on her, not her pregnancy.&quot;</p> <p>Even if you feel fine, it&#x27;s still best to call your healthcare provider as soon as possible to describe your accident, discuss any symptoms you&#x27;re having, and talk about potential next steps. They may direct you to the ER or their offices to be checked out.</p> <p>&quot;I was in a car accident at 27 weeks,&quot; shares one mom in the <a href="https://community.babycenter.com/">BabyCenter Community</a>. &quot;In that case, I had to go in for monitoring and even still everything was fine.&quot;</p><div class="cmsAdContainer mui-fx7q5a"><div id="primisOuter" class="mobileMarginProvider" style="min-height:202px"><div></div></div></div><div data-transform="embeddedRegPromo"> </div> <h2 id="what-can-happen-if-im-in-a-car-accident-while-pregnant">What can happen if I&#x27;m in a car accident while pregnant?</h2> <p>With car accidents or any kind of jarring impact while you&#x27;re pregnant, the main cause for concern is <a href="/pregnancy/health-and-safety/placental-abruption_1425791">placental abruption</a>, when the placenta separates from the inner wall of your uterus before delivery. The womb offers some protection for your baby and placenta during a sudden impact, such as slamming on the brakes; but even if the jolt didn&#x27;t feel that severe, it can still potentially separate the placenta from the uterus.</p> <p>Placental abruption is rare in any case except a serious accident, but it&#x27;s important to rule out because it can lead to serious problems including <a href="/pregnancy/health-and-safety/subchorionic-bleeding-during-pregnancy_40005840">hemorrhage</a>, <a href="/pregnancy/health-and-safety/miscarriage-signs-causes-and-treatment_252">miscarriage</a>, or <a href="/pregnancy/health-and-safety/preterm-labor-and-birth_1055">premature labor</a>. Yet even with an abruption, you may not notice any symptoms. That&#x27;s why it&#x27;s important to have an exam to verify that you&#x27;re okay and your baby is, too.</p> <p>&quot;I got into an accident where someone hit me from behind,&quot; shares another mom in the Community. &quot;It wasn&#x27;t bad at all, but my husband said I should go anyway because you just never know. I&#x27;m not trying to scare [anyone], but I had a small rupture in my placenta which caused me to bleed… When it came time for labor, I bled very heavily, which was really scary. Always get checked after getting into an accident.&quot;</p><div class="cmsAdContainer mui-fx7q5a"><div class="greyAdBox nativeAdContainer mui-1qwcnd7"><div class="advertisementLabel grey">Advertisement | page continues below</div><div class="adSlotContainer grey"></div></div></div> <p>Once you’re home, it&#x27;s normal to feel extra alert to every twinge. The <a href="https://babycenter.onelink.me/nZLz/e9beyzbt">BabyCenter app</a> can help you keep track of symptoms, monitor your baby’s movements, and get clear, medically reviewed guidance on what deserves a call to your provider. Peace of mind matters – especially after something scary.</p> <h2 id="what-will-happen-at-the-hospital-after-a-car-accident">What will happen at the hospital after a car accident?</h2> <p>In the ER, you&#x27;ll be checked out and, once your overall condition is stable, you&#x27;ll receive a thorough obstetric exam and ultrasound to check on your baby and placenta. Depending on how far along you are and any symptoms you have, such as bleeding or contractions, you and your baby may be monitored for several hours or more. If you&#x27;re <a href="/pregnancy/health-and-safety/rh-factor_1480">Rh-negative</a>, you may get a shot of Rh immunoglobulin if there&#x27;s any chance that your blood has mixed with your baby&#x27;s.</p> <p>&quot;If there is direct abdominal contact, i.e. with a seatbelt, 24 hours of monitoring in the hospital is likely in the second and third trimesters,&quot; Dr. Amaru says.</p> <p>After you receive the okay to go home, you&#x27;ll need to keep an eye out for <a href="/pregnancy/your-body/vaginal-bleeding-or-spotting-during-pregnancy_3081">vaginal bleeding</a>, <a href="/pregnancy/health-and-safety/leaking-amniotic-fluid-premature-rupture-of-membranes_40007984">leaking fluid</a>, contractions or other <a href="/pregnancy/your-body/cramps-during-pregnancy_204">cramping or abdominal pain</a>, or a change in your baby&#x27;s movement. If you experience any of these symptoms, call your healthcare provider right away.</p> <p>This can be an emotionally difficult and scary time, so it’s important to take care of your mental health after an accident, too, by reaching out to trusted family members, friends, or a counselor if you want to talk about how you&#x27;re feeling.</p><div class="cmsAdContainer mui-fx7q5a"><div class="greyAdBox mui-1qwcnd7"><div class="advertisementLabel grey">Advertisement | page continues below</div><div class="adSlotContainer grey"></div></div></div> <h2 id="safety-tips-for-driving-while-pregnant">Safety tips for driving while pregnant</h2> <ul> <li><strong>Always wear a properly fastened seat belt throughout pregnancy.</strong> This means ensuring that a three-point seatbelt, complete with both the lap and shoulder straps, are in <a href="/pregnancy/health-and-safety/is-it-safe-to-use-a-seat-belt-during-pregnancy_7239">the proper location</a>. Don&#x27;t wrap the lap belt above or in front of your belly.</li> <li><strong>Don&#x27;t text and drive.</strong> While it may seem like sending a quick text is harmless, it’s easy to be distracted and not see an accident that could have been avoided. Other distracted driving that can lead to an accident includes eating, feeding a child or finding a toy for them, using your GPS, changing the music, and putting on makeup.</li> <li><strong>Make sure your car&#x27;s airbags work.</strong> Try to keep 10 inches between the steering wheel and your breastbone. Consider angling the steering wheel, if it tilts, toward your breastbone rather than your belly or your head.</li> <li><strong>Keep emergency contact information with you.</strong> This can be something as simple as a card in your wallet that lets first responders know who to call if need be.</li> <li><strong>Consider a pregnancy ID bracelet.</strong> This should be engraved with your due date, your blood type, and any other medical information that would be crucial for an ER physician to know in case of an emergency.</li> </ul> <p>A car accident during pregnancy is scary – even if it’s minor. Trust your instincts, get checked out, and remember: Taking care of yourself is the very best way to take care of your baby.</p></div></div><div id="articleFeedbackModule" class="mui-17925eo"><div class="feedbackSection"><div class="feedbackTitle"><div>Was this article helpful?</div></div><div class="feedbackIcons"><div class="articleWasHelpful"><img src="https://assets.babycenter.com/ims/2022/11/ic_articleFeedback_smile.svg" alt="" role="presentation" width="32" height="32" class="spacerRight100" loading="lazy"/><div>Yes</div></div><div class="articleWasNotHelpful"><img src="https://assets.babycenter.com/ims/2022/11/ic_articleFeedback_meh.svg" alt="" role="presentation" width="32" height="32" class="spacerRight100" loading="l